{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat control-reader-brief.md controls/control-packet-6.json'","aggregated_output":"# Stage B: reading the control theses (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs, and do not read any file other than this brief\nand the one packet you are given.\n\nEach item of your packet pairs a **ruler question** (a question written by hand for an earlier, frozen measurement, with its\nunit, due day and ladder of rungs) with a **judged question** the system wrote itself for a thesis it opened (its question, the\noutcome it looks at, due day, year and rungs). It also lists **reader B's decisive items**: the evidence an earlier reader, called\nreader B, cited for the frozen answer to the ruler question (each with its day, title, passage and meaning), and **the evidence\nshown to the judge**: every evidence entry the system's judge was shown for the judged question in any of its requests on or\nbefore 2026-07-14 (empty when there is none). You are not told either answer, and you do not judge which answer is right.\n\nFor each item, answer three questions.\n\n1. `same_question`: **yes** when the judged question asks what the ruler question asks: the same quantity measured the same way\n   (the same total, scope and unit: not a part of it, a ceiling on it, another quantity or another unit), or the same event,\n   for the same period. **no** otherwise. Wording may differ; a judged question that asks for more or less than the ruler's,\n   or for another period, is **no**.\n2. `same_due`: for an **event** (a yes-or-no question), **yes** only when the judged question's deadline is the ruler's\n   deadline. For a **figure**, **yes** when the two due days both mark the publication of the same period's figure (for\n   example, both the annual report of the same fiscal year, even if the two days differ); **no** otherwise.\n3. `items`: for **each** of reader B's decisive items, by its id, **shown** when its content is present in the evidence shown to\n   the judge (the same fact or figure, in any wording, from any source), **not_shown** when it is absent. Search the entries for\n   the figure or the fact; do not mark an item shown because a related item is.\n\nReturn one JSON object per item, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else (no prose, no code fence):\n`{\"control\": 3, \"same_question\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"same_due\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"items\": {\"i005\": \"shown\"|\"not_shown\", ...}, \"why\": \"one sentence naming what decides each no and each not_shown\"}`.\n\n## Third reader (only where the two readers differ)\n\nA third reader of each family is given this brief, the packet, and a list of the disputed points, each a control number and a\nfield (`same_question`, `same_due`, or `item <id>`), without the two readers' answers. For each point, answer that one question\nafresh by the rules above and return one JSON object per point, one per line, and nothing else:\n`{\"control\": 3, \"field\": \"item i005\", \"ruling\": \"yes\"|\"no\"|\"shown\"|\"not_shown\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}` (`yes`/`no` for the two\nquestion fields, `shown`/`not_shown` for an item).\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 11,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2028?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-02-28\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $650 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 650000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $550 billion and below $650 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 550000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 650000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $480 billion and below $550 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 480000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $420 billion and below $480 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 420000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 480000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is below $420 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 420000000000.0\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n   \"question\": \"What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2028?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2028\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2028-02-29\",\n   \"year\": \"FY2028\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $625 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 625000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $554 billion and below $625 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 554000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 625000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $500 billion and below $554 billion, below the cited analyst estimate.\",\n    \"lo\": 500000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 554000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is at least $400 billion and below $500 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 400000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 500000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2028 total revenue is below $400 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 400000000000.0\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i037\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-02\",\n    \"title\": \"Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?\",\n    \"passage\": \"from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts\",\n     \"what\": \"NVIDIA total revenue estimates\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$392 billion this fiscal year, $554 billion next year (from $253 billion trailing twelve months)\",\n     \"period\": \"this fiscal year (FY2027) and the following year (FY2028)\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n     \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n     \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n     \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n     \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i079\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-03\",\n    \"title\": \"Here's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself\",\n    \"passage\": \"In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Jensen Huang\",\n     \"what\": \"Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$1 trillion\",\n     \"period\": \"March 2026 to end-2027\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i100\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n     \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n     \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n     \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e24 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\",\n   \"- e32 [fact; Ben Wodecki, SDxCentral; original source Microsoft and MediaTek ; from gnews:1740f3733eedc34d8bac9b65 2026-07-03] Microsoft and MediaTek plan to launch microLED-based data-center networking interconnects for partners by late 2027, following a collaboration to develop Microsoft’s Mosaic prototype system.\\n    Quote: Those efforts were initially detailed in a paper published last September, but have since matured via a collaboration with Taiwanese fabless semiconductor company MediaTek. The pair plans to launch microLED-based data center networking interconnects for partners by late 2027 .\",\n   \"- e34 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, The Motley Fool, recounting Jensen Huang’s statement; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang expected Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales from March 2026 through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\",\n   \"- e35 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, The Motley Fool, recounting NVIDIA management’s statement; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA announced plans in FY2027 Q1 to expand in CPUs, and management said it expected nearly $20 billion in CPU sales; the item gives no period for that sales estimate.\\n    Quote: Furthermore, in its first fiscal quarter of 2027, Nvidia announced plans to become much more involved in the central processing unit (CPU) space , which has been in high demand due to the rise of agentic AI. Management said they think they can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales, making it an instant industry leader.\",\n   \"- e36 [third_party_view; Bram Berkowitz, The Motley Fool, recounting Cerebras’ claim; original source Cerebras ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] Cerebras claimed that its chips were 15 times faster than some NVIDIA GPU models.\\n    Quote: There are competitors like Cerebras and Space Exploration Technologies Corp , which have promised to make more powerful GPUs than Nvidia, and Cerebras already has chips it claims are 15 times faster than some of Nvidia's models.\",\n   \"- e24 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e49; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e50 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Wall Street analysts project NVIDIA revenue growth of 41% for FY2028.\\n    Quote: and 41% next year\",\n   \"- e97 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] Wall Street analysts expected NVIDIA growth of 41% in 2027.\\n    Quote: For 2027, that figure rises to 41%.\",\n   \"- e118 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Jensen Huang said the cost of a 1-gigawatt AI factory has risen from $20–30 billion to $50–60 billion and could reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt soon, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e166 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey said Chinese executives expect domestic products’ share of their AI-accelerator budgets to rise to 46% over the next 12 months from 30% today.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\",\n   \"- e168 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A large pool of respondents to the Bloomberg Intelligence survey was evaluating AI accelerators from Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies.\\n    Quote: AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\",\n   \"- e169 [third_party_view; Bloomberg Intelligence report, quoted by businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg Intelligence said China’s substitution of locally made AI semiconductors for foreign ones was making progress and was likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon; its survey covered 60 executives.\\n    Quote: “China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey expected Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips became harder to find and local firms took over, noting Beijing had urged Chinese technology firms not to use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\",\n   \"- e171 [fact; businesstimes.com.sg; original source China ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] China is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centers over the next five years as part of a government-led AI expansion initiative.\\n    Quote: China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\",\n   \"- e172 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies would supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China’s data-center initiative.\\n    Quote: At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\",\n   \"- e173 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence report ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] The report said a global memory-chip shortage was likely to cap growth at Chinese AI firms such as SMIC and that bottlenecks were shifting toward securing high-bandwidth memory supply.\\n    Quote: But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits.\",\n   \"- e186 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author says NVIDIA sales will likely accelerate as data-center spending shifts from construction costs to computing costs.\\n    Quote: There's a very strong correlation between increasing data center spending and Nvidia's sales, and that trend will likely accelerate as data centers shift from construction costs to computing costs.\",\n   \"- e187 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author expects Microsoft and TSMC to report strong earnings and forward guidance, which the author says could lift NVIDIA shares.\\n    Quote: I think both of these companies will report strong earnings with great forward guidance, and that could cause Nvidia's stock to rise.\",\n   \"- e188 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author says NVIDIA's core clients have not shifted their actual spending habits and expects NVIDIA to report a strong quarter in late August.\\n    Quote: Even if it doesn't, Nvidia reports earnings in late August and will likely report another blowout quarter, as there hasn't been any shift in actual spending habits from Nvidia's core clients yet.\",\n   \"- e190 [third_party_view; fool.com (Jack Delaney); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] SpaceX plans to use Nvidia chips in its AI1 orbital data-center satellites, with Nvidia set to be an initial hardware supplier; no schedule or amount was given.\\n    Quote: That involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips.\",\n   \"- e24 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e49, e226; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey expected Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips became harder to find and local firms took over, noting Beijing had urged Chinese technology firms not to use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e243; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e172 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies would supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China’s data-center initiative.\\n    Quote: At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e244; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e233 [fact; Futuriom; original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported that Meta was setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models on July 1, 2026.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models.\",\n   \"- e34 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, The Motley Fool, recounting Jensen Huang’s statement; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang expected Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales from March 2026 through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e281; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e281 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear customer commitments.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e287 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Bloomberg Intelligence projects NVIDIA will hold 70%–75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\\n    Quote: Bloomberg Intelligence projects the company will hold 70% to 75% of the AI chip market through 2030.\",\n   \"- e290 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta plans to build three more MTIA chip generations over the next two years to run generative AI models.\\n    Quote: Meta is set to build three more generations over the next two years with the purpose of running generative AI models.\",\n   \"- e291 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source QZ.com 2026-07-09; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta's MTIA 450 is expected to arrive in early 2027.\\n    Quote: The next one, MTIA 450, arrives in early 2027, doubling how fast the chip moves data in and out of memory.\",\n   \"- e292 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source QZ.com 2026-07-09; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Meta's MTIA 500 is expected to arrive later in 2027.\\n    Quote: Its eventual successor, MTIA 500, is expected to come later next year, and pushes memory speed another 50% higher.\",\n   \"- e294 [third_party_view; QZ.com; original source Reuters ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla completed the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026 and plans to produce it at TSMC's Arizona plant and Samsung's Texas plant.\\n    Quote: Tesla finished the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026, according to Reuters . Production is planned at both TSMC 's plant in Arizona and Samsung's plant in Texas.\",\n   \"- e295 [party_statement; QZ.com; original source Tesla ; from gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09] Tesla is targeting mid-2027 to begin mass production of its AI5 chip.\\n    Quote: Tesla is targeting mid-2027 for mass production.\",\n   \"- e302 [party_statement; au.finance.yahoo.com; original source Cerebras ; from gnews:66cb0387229ac4b1390615cb 2026-07-09] Cerebras plans to build a European AI data-centre network with 200 MW of combined power capacity by the end of 2027, expanding compute capacity in a market served by NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The company plans to build a network of AI data centres across Europe by the end of 2027, with a combined power capacity of 200 MW.\",\n   \"- e303 [party_statement; Andrew Feldman, quoted by au.finance.yahoo.com; original source Andrew Feldman, Cerebras CEO ; from gnews:66cb0387229ac4b1390615cb 2026-07-09] Cerebras CEO Andrew Feldman said the company’s European AI data-centre expansions are worth several billion dollars.\\n    Quote: \\\"These are massive expansions\\\" worth several billion dollars, chief executive Andrew Feldman told AFP on the sidelines of the RAISE Summit in Paris.\",\n   \"- e306 [third_party_view; Trefis analyst; original source Wall Street analyst consensus ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] Trefis reported analyst consensus forecasts NVIDIA revenue growth of about 32% annually over the next few years.\\n    Quote: Wall Street consensus sees NVIDIA’s revenue growing about 32% a year for the next few years.\",\n   \"- e170 [third_party_view; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey expected Nvidia’s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips became harder to find and local firms took over, noting Beijing had urged Chinese technology firms not to use H20 chips.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e243, e386; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e339 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts estimated that NVIDIA held more than 90% of the AI training-chip market in 2025.\\n    Quote: According to Silicon Analysts , which compiles estimates from TrendForce, Morgan Stanley $MS , and TSMC $TSM capacity data, Nvidia 's share exceeded 90% in training in 2025.\",\n   \"- e340 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts estimated NVIDIA's AI inference-chip market share at 60%–75% in 2025, amid competition from custom silicon.\\n    Quote: Its inference share sat between 60% and 75% over the same period, undercut by growing competition from custom silicon.\",\n   \"- e341 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source TechCrunch ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] TechCrunch reported that 1.4 million Amazon Trainium chips were deployed across three generations as of March, including more than one million Trainium2 chips running Anthropic's Claude.\\n    Quote: As of March, 1.4 million Trainium chips were deployed across three generations, according to TechCrunch , with Anthropic's Claude running on more than one million Trainium2 chips alone.\",\n   \"- e342 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Amazon committed 2 GW of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI.\\n    Quote: Amazon has also committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI, putting its chips inside two of the industry's most prominent AI labs.\",\n   \"- e343 [fact; qz.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] AMD launched ROCm 7 and a free developer cloud.\\n    Quote: AMD launched ROCm 7 and a free developer cloud.\",\n   \"- e344 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Mizuho Securities and Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Mizuho Securities estimated NVIDIA's overall AI-chip market share at 70%–95% depending on segment; Silicon Analysts projected it would settle near 75% as the total market grows beyond $200 billion.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's overall AI chip market share, estimated by Mizuho Securities at between 70% and 95% depending on segment, is projected by Silicon Analysts to settle near 75% as the total market expands past $200 billion.\",\n   \"- e348 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI starting late in 2026 and continuing into 2027.\\n    Quote: However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\",\n   \"- e349 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-12; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] The author says the market expects NVIDIA to maintain its lead in growth for the full fiscal year, while Broadcom narrows the gap.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, the market still expects Nvidia to maintain its lead for the full fiscal year, though Broadcom is starting to close the gap.\",\n   \"- e359 [party_statement; tech-insider.org, reporting NVIDIA’s statement; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs in 2027–2028.\\n    Quote: The same AI boom that has made Nvidia one of the most valuable companies on the planet is now directly limiting what it can sell to the gamers who built its original fanbase. Every HBM stack that goes into a data center accelerator is capacity that does not go into a GeForce card, and Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\",\n   \"- e385 [third_party_view; barchart.com (author), citing a Bloomberg survey; original source Bloomberg survey respondents ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] A Bloomberg survey found Chinese technology companies expect domestic suppliers to receive about 46% of their AI-chip spending over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\\n    Quote: A new Bloomberg survey showed Chinese technology companies expect to allocate roughly 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e166; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e386 [third_party_view; barchart.com (author); original source barchart.com (author) 2026-07-12; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] The author said Beijing has encouraged companies to reduce purchases of foreign AI processors, adding pressure to NVIDIA’s long-term growth in China.\\n    Quote: Beijing has also encouraged companies to reduce purchases of foreign AI processors, creating additional pressure on Nvidia's long-term growth in one of its largest overseas markets.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e393 [party_statement; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] NVIDIA expects sovereign AI projects and on-premise deployments to account for a larger share of future growth; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: It expects sovereign AI projects and on-premise deployments to make up a larger share of future growth as AI adoption expands.\",\n   \"- e395 [third_party_view; fool.com (Sean Williams); original source Sean Williams 2026-07-13; from gnews:e7e8cbf33738ceb5865837f0 2026-07-13] The author argues that GPU supply shortages support NVIDIA’s pricing power and gross margin, and that customer-developed chips occupying data-center capacity could erode that advantage.\\n    Quote: For starters, some of Nvidia's pricing power and otherworldly gross margin originates from persistent GPU supply shortages. If the company's top customers are developing chips that take up valuable data center real estate, Nvidia can quickly lose its biggest tailwind .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e267; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e396 [third_party_view; fool.com (Sean Williams); original source Sean Williams 2026-07-13; from gnews:e7e8cbf33738ceb5865837f0 2026-07-13] The author says hyperscalers’ internally developed AI chips are less costly and presumably more available than NVIDIA GPUs, whose demand has created a sizable backlog.\\n    Quote: Secondly, these internally developed chips are notably less costly than Nvidia's hardware and, presumably, more readily available (a function of demand creating a sizable backlog for Nvidia's GPUs).\",\n   \"- e397 [third_party_view; fool.com (Sean Williams); original source Sean Williams 2026-07-13; from gnews:e7e8cbf33738ceb5865837f0 2026-07-13] The author argues that cost-effective in-house AI chips at NVIDIA’s top customers may delay hyperscalers’ data-center upgrades and future NVIDIA GPU purchases.\\n    Quote: Lastly, AI chips developed by Nvidia's top customers may delay future upgrade cycles. Between Nvidia's accelerated innovation cycle, which is depreciating prior-generation GPUs, and the company's top customers developing cost-effective in-house solutions, hyperscalers may push out AI data center upgrades.\",\n   \"- e400 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Google and Blackstone ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google and Blackstone are working on a cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms in 2027, potentially adding competition for NVIDIA cloud demand.\\n    Quote: Google is also working with Blackstone on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms next year.\",\n   \"- e421 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125–145 billion and launched Meta Compute, a service to rent excess internal AI capacity to enterprises.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e233 (2026-07-01); both are in force: Bloomberg reported that Meta was setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models on July 1, 2026.\",\n   \"- e425 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley said hyperscalers continue expanding proprietary-silicon investment to lower total cost of ownership and reduce reliance on merchant GPU suppliers.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley says hyperscalers continue to expand their investments in proprietary silicon to improve total cost of ownership and reduce dependence on merchant GPU suppliers.\",\n   \"- e427 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimated that one gigawatt of AI infrastructure costs $6 billion–$11 billion with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks, versus roughly $19 billion with Nvidia GB300-based racks.\\n    Quote: Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks.\",\n   \"- e428 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimated that rack costs using Vera Rubin would be $25 billion.\\n    Quote: Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion.\",\n   \"- e24 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e49, e226, e437, e438; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e281 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear customer commitments.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34, e436; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e344 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Mizuho Securities and Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Mizuho Securities estimated NVIDIA's overall AI-chip market share at 70%–95% depending on segment; Silicon Analysts projected it would settle near 75% as the total market grows beyond $200 billion.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's overall AI chip market share, estimated by Mizuho Securities at between 70% and 95% depending on segment, is projected by Silicon Analysts to settle near 75% as the total market expands past $200 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e444; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e421 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125–145 billion and launched Meta Compute, a service to rent excess internal AI capacity to enterprises.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e460; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e233 (2026-07-01); both are in force: Bloomberg reported that Meta was setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models on July 1, 2026.\",\n   \"- e436 [party_statement; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from the Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e281; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e437 [third_party_view; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of approximately $555 billion for FY2028, ending January 2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia will generate approximately $555 billion in revenue for the company's next fiscal year, ending January 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e438 [third_party_view; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source fool.com author Justin Pope 2026-07-14; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] The author cites NVIDIA trailing 12-month revenue of $253 billion as the base for a forecast that sales could roughly double within two years.\\n    Quote: In other words, sales could roughly double within the next two years, based on Nvidia's trailing 12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e439 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author, reporting TSMC's guidance; original source TSMC ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] TSMC raised its forecast for global semiconductor-market revenue in 2030 from $1 trillion to $1.5 trillion, citing AI-fueled chip demand.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's foundry partner TSMC recently noted that the global semiconductor market's revenue could reach a whopping $1.5 trillion in 2030. The Taiwan-based foundry giant had previously anticipated $1 trillion in semiconductor revenue by the end of the decade. However, AI-fueled demand for chips led to a substantial upgrade to its guidance.\",\n   \"- e440 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting TSMC's estimate; original source TSMC ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] TSMC expects AI and high-performance-computing chips to represent 55% of the projected 2030 global semiconductor-market opportunity.\\n    Quote: TSMC points out that AI and high-performance computing (HPC) chips will account for 55% of this lucrative opportunity.\",\n   \"- e441 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2026 data-center revenue of $193.7 billion.\\n    Quote: For comparison, Nvidia's data center revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year) was $193.7 billion.\",\n   \"- e442 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 data-center revenue included $162.3 billion from compute-chip sales, with the remainder from networking components.\\n    Quote: It is worth noting that $162.3 billion of its fiscal 2026 data center revenue came from sales of compute chips, while the rest was from networking components.\",\n   \"- e443 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source Analysts ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Analysts believe NVIDIA's share of the AI data-center chip market may have peaked.\\n    Quote: However, analysts believe that Nvidia's AI data center chip market share may have peaked.\",\n   \"- e444 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting analysts' estimate; original source Analysts ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Analysts anticipate NVIDIA's AI-chip market share will decline to 75% in 2026.\\n    Quote: That's why Nvidia's AI chip market share is anticipated to decline to 75% this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e344; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e445 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author estimates that NVIDIA could generate more than $400 billion in 2030 data-center chip revenue if it has 50% market share of an $825 billion market.\\n    Quote: Let's assume Nvidia continues to lose ground in AI chips for the next four years and ends up at just 50% market share in 2030; it can still generate more than $400 billion in data center chip revenue in 2030 (based on the $825 billion market size estimated above).\",\n   \"- e446 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2026 networking revenue of $31.4 billion, up 142% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company reported a 142% year-over-year increase in networking revenue in fiscal 2026 to $31.4 billion.\",\n   \"- e447 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter networking revenue of $14.8 billion, triple the year-earlier amount.\\n    Quote: It has started fiscal 2027 on a stronger note in this segment, with networking revenue tripling year-over-year to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e448 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting Mordor Intelligence's forecast; original source Mordor Intelligence ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Mordor Intelligence expects the InfiniBand market to grow 36% annually over the next five years and exceed $164 billion in revenue in 2031.\\n    Quote: The InfiniBand market, for instance, is expected to clock 36% annual growth over the next five years, according to Mordor Intelligence. It could generate more than $164 billion in revenue in 2031.\",\n   \"- e449 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting Dell'Oro Group's forecast; original source Dell'Oro Group ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Dell'Oro Group projects that the data-center switch market will exceed $100 billion in revenue by 2030.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the data center switch market is projected to exceed $100 billion in revenue by 2030, according to Dell'Oro Group.\",\n   \"- e450 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author estimates NVIDIA's combined data-center compute and networking addressable opportunity could exceed $1 trillion by 2030.\\n    Quote: In all, Nvidia's data center addressable opportunity, including both networking and compute, could surpass $1 trillion by the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e451 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author says consensus revenue-growth projections for NVIDIA through fiscal 2029 have increased significantly.\\n    Quote: That's why there has been a significant jump in Nvidia's consensus revenue growth projections through fiscal 2029.\",\n   \"- e453 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter 2026-07-08; from gnews:a0b0f251bbab383483ec679b 2026-07-14] TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter said NVIDIA’s integrated hardware and software platform gives it an underappreciated competitive edge in the next AI phase, that AI spending is durable across applications and customers, and that industry-specific AI deployments will become a much larger growth driver over time.\\n    Quote: The analyst opines that NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) possesses an underappreciated competitive edge in the next phase of AI. This is because it provides an integrated hardware and software platform, which works seamlessly together. The analyst remains confident that AI spending is durable throughout different applications and customers.\",\n   \"- e506 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of NVIDIA’s latest-generation GPUs in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 to provide AI computing capacity.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\",\n   \"- e507 [third_party_view; Jakarta Globe, citing The Straits Times; original source The Straits Times ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] The Straits Times reported that the Batam data center project is projected to generate up to $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Quote: According to The Straits Times , the project is projected to generate as much as $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 12,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Below $550 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is less than $550 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$550 billion to below $600 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $550 billion and less than $600 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 550000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 600000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$600 billion to below $700 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $600 billion and less than $700 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 600000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 700000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$700 billion to below $800 billion\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 700000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": 800000000000.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"$800 billion or more\",\n     \"condition\": \"Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\",\n     \"lo\": 800000000000.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-02-28\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Below $550 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined capital expenditures are below $550 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 550000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"$550 billion to below $625 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined capital expenditures are at least $550 billion and below $625 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 550000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 625000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"$625 billion to below $675 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined capital expenditures are at least $625 billion and below $675 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 625000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 675000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"$675 billion to below $750 billion\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined capital expenditures are at least $675 billion and below $750 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 675000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": 750000000000.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"$750 billion or more\",\n    \"condition\": \"Combined capital expenditures are at least $750 billion.\",\n    \"lo\": 750000000000.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i256\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n    \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center ca