ng that while its share within Meta may decline, overall AI capital expenditures from major cloud service providers are expected to rise significantly.\",\n   \"- e353 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source tech-insider.org 2026-07-12; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron reportedly sold out their 2026 HBM output to AI data-center customers.\\n    Quote: The cause is a global memory shortage: Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have reportedly sold out their 2026 HBM output to AI data center customers.\",\n   \"- e358 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org, reporting Micron and Kearney’s PERLab; original source Micron and Kearney’s PERLab ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Micron suggested memory supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027 and may gradually return to balance by 2028; Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projected the shortage could last into 2030.\\n    Quote: Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 .\",\n   \"- e421 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125–145 billion and launched Meta Compute, a service to rent excess internal AI capacity to enterprises.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e233 (2026-07-01); both are in force: Bloomberg reported that Meta was setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models on July 1, 2026.\",\n   \"- e421 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to $125–145 billion and launched Meta Compute, a service to rent excess internal AI capacity to enterprises.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion, then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e460; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e233 (2026-07-01); both are in force: Bloomberg reported that Meta was setting up a cloud-based business to sell AI compute and models on July 1, 2026.\",\n   \"- e434 [third_party_view; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] The author says Meta Platforms, Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon are planning higher capital expenditures in calendar 2026; no amount is given.\\n    Quote: Hyperscalers, including Meta Platforms , Microsoft , Alphabet , and Amazon , are planning higher capital expenditures in 2026.\",\n   \"- e435 [third_party_view; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates AI compute spending at approximately $494 billion in calendar 2026, rising to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\",\n   \"- e460 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja 2026-07-14; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] The author cited Meta’s $145 billion infrastructure budget, partly aimed at custom silicon, as a potential competitive threat to NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The Reddit crowd has fixated on competitive threats, from DeepSeek’s rumored in-house chip to Meta’s $145B infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon.\\n    Opi